  <heading level="2">Multiply a Binomial by a Binomial</heading>
  <paragraph>Just like there are different ways to represent multiplication of numbers, there are several methods that can be used to multiply a binomial times a binomial. We will start by using the Distributive Property.</paragraph>
  <heading level="3">Multiply a Binomial by a Binomial Using the Distributive Property</heading>
  <paragraph>Look at Example 6.33, where we multiplied a binomial by a monomial.</paragraph>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>We distributed the p to get:</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>What if we have (x + 7) instead of p?</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Distribute (x + 7).</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Distribute again.</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
        <cell/>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <paragraph>Notice that before combining like terms, you had four terms. You multiplied the two terms of the first binomial by the two terms of the second binomial—four multiplications.</paragraph>
